thout actually using it, though this doesn't trigger any lint violations given the project's configuration. The rest of the code quality looks solid—proper TypeScript types throughout, correct enum usage, and clean import paths.\n\nOne more thing about the grid tag: it declares `dataType: AttributeDataType.Number` but the values array contains strings like `['1', '2', ...]`. The type checking uses `parseFloat` and `isFinite` so string numbers pass validation, and the allowed values rule compares against those string representations, so it's internally consistent. This is actually a pre-existing condition—the values were already strings before the PR, which just added the Number dataType declaration.\n\nAll the validation rules match the expected signature and are properly registered. Diagnostic ranges point to the full tag in most cases, with `validateEmptyRequiredAttributeValue` using more precise ranges for individual attributes. Severity levels are appropriate—errors for empty required attributes, warnings for others—and the messages are clear and actionable. No changes needed for completions, decorations, grammar, or testing.\n\nI've written 340 lines of tests covering all five rules with positive, negative, and edge cases using Mocha and assert, and they're all passing. There are a couple of minor cleanup items: an orphaned diagnostic file for image attribute conflicts that's no longer used after the card definition was refactored, and three rule files that import `TagValidationContext` unnecessarily alongside `TagValidationRuleFn`.\n\nBoth findings are suggestions rather than blockers, so I'm ready to mark this as approved and wrap up.",
