        "reasoning_text": "Good. So for `number` type, the checker validates `!isNaN(parseFloat(actualTrimmedValue)) && isFinite(Number(actualTrimmedValue))`. So when the grid has `dataType: Number` and `values: ['1', '2', ...]`, the values are strings but represent valid numbers. The `checkTagAttributeType` would accept them since `parseFloat('1')` is `1` and `isFinite(Number('1'))` is true. And the `validateTagAttributeAllowedValues` converts all values to strings via `new Set(attrDef.values.map(String))`. \n\nBoth the grid and cell validation work because the type checker accepts string representations of numbers and the allowed values validator normalizes everything to strings. There's an inconsistency though—the grid uses string values while the cell uses numeric values—but since both get converted to strings during validation, it doesn't cause any issues.\n\nThe PR only updated the `dataType` to match the actual numeric values, which is the right call. No real problem there.\n\nLet me wrap up my findings. The code is solid overall. I found one unused import in the validateDuplicateAttributes rule file—`findTagAttributeNameRange` is imported but never actually used since the rule relies on `tagInstance.tagRange` instead. It's dead code that could be cleaned up, though TypeScript isn't flagging it since `noUnusedLocals` isn't enabled.\n\nThere's also an orphaned file from the card tag migration—`imageAttrConflict.ts` was replaced by the declarative `mutuallyExclusiveWith` approach, and while the import was removed from `card.types.ts`, the file itself is still sitting in the codebase as dead code.\n\nFinally, there's an inconsistency in how diagnostic messages are formatted between positional and named attributes in the validateEmptyRequiredAttributeValue rule. Positional attributes skip the \"attribute\" word and quotes, while named attributes include them, creating an uneven message style.\n\nThe duplicate attribute regex has a potential false-positive issue where it could match patterns inside quoted values—like if a URL contains `source=test`, the regex might incorrectly flag `source` as a duplicate attribute. While KFM values rarely have this pattern, query parameters could trigger it, and the regex could be tightened to skip quoted strings, though that would require more work.\n\nAll findings are suggestions only, no blocking issues. The PR looks solid overall—the five rules follow the established pattern correctly, the de-duplication logic is sound, the dataType additions align with the attribute semantics, tests are thorough with 340 lines of coverage, and everything passes. The migration from the custom card rule to this declarative approach is clean. I'll write up the artifacts and post the summary now.",
